WEBVTT

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:03.775
A shopper orders eighty dollars of
merchandise.

00:00:03.775 --> 00:00:07.700
The parcel arrives late, and she sends
back two items.

00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:13.050
The shop follows Microsoft’s Dynamics 365
Commerce architecture.

00:00:13.050 --> 00:00:15.875
Order one oh four and the Foundry service
that helps

00:00:15.875 --> 00:00:19.000
with her return are examples we’ve added
to it.

00:00:20.558 --> 00:00:22.583
At checkout, the storefront sends the
order

00:00:22.583 --> 00:00:26.133
to the Commerce Scale Unit, the engine
behind the store.

00:00:26.133 --> 00:00:28.058
Pull back to the whole shop.

00:00:28.058 --> 00:00:33.008
This version of its master graph has
forty-six entities and sixty-eight links.

00:00:33.008 --> 00:00:35.058
Each link says something specific,

00:00:35.058 --> 00:00:38.608
such as who ships a parcel or which system
looks up an order.

00:00:40.133 --> 00:00:45.333
A packing list and a financial report can
both refer to order one oh four.

00:00:45.333 --> 00:00:49.333
A person looking after the whole shop
needs a different view again.

00:00:49.333 --> 00:00:51.883
Which parts of the business depend on each
other,

00:00:51.883 --> 00:00:54.583
and what would explain a broken link
between them?

00:00:56.508 --> 00:00:59.183
The warehouse hands parcels to a carrier.

00:00:59.183 --> 00:01:01.758
The refund worker reads a case record.

00:01:01.758 --> 00:01:04.433
Both links belong to the same master
graph,

00:01:04.433 --> 00:01:07.908
along with the services and records for
the rest of the shop.

00:01:07.908 --> 00:01:10.733
Their verbs tell us what each connection
means.

00:01:12.258 --> 00:01:16.533
A projection can group the whole shop into
fourteen business activities.

00:01:16.533 --> 00:01:19.058
The refund worker belongs to Payout.

00:01:19.058 --> 00:01:23.183
The case store and the approval record
belong to Returns Intake.

00:01:23.183 --> 00:01:26.583
The worker reads the store and requires
the approval.

00:01:26.583 --> 00:01:28.958
Both links carry up to the business view.

00:01:28.958 --> 00:01:32.008
The white paper calls these levels of
abstraction.

00:01:33.558 --> 00:01:36.958
In a tree view, Returns Intake appears
under Payout

00:01:36.958 --> 00:01:38.833
and under Customer Support.

00:01:38.833 --> 00:01:41.608
Both entries point to one part of the
shop.

00:01:41.608 --> 00:01:45.508
Under Payout the evidence still says which
record the worker reads

00:01:45.508 --> 00:01:47.083
and which it requires.

00:01:47.083 --> 00:01:50.058
Only the approval link declares a
requirement.

00:01:50.058 --> 00:01:53.658
A read alone cannot tell us that a failure
will block payment.

00:01:55.592 --> 00:01:59.092
Checkout records order one oh four and
reserves the items

00:01:59.092 --> 00:02:02.092
so another shopper can’t buy the same
stock.

00:02:02.092 --> 00:02:08.392
Dynamics 365 Supply Chain Management
covers the warehouse and transportation.

00:02:08.392 --> 00:02:10.717
The packer uses the order lines.

00:02:10.717 --> 00:02:12.842
The carrier assigns a tracking number that

00:02:12.842 --> 00:02:15.542
ties delivery updates back to the order.

00:02:17.075 --> 00:02:20.350
A scan from the carrier changes the
delivery date.

00:02:20.350 --> 00:02:23.200
That one event can serve several purposes.

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:26.350
Support wants to answer a question about
the parcel.

00:02:26.350 --> 00:02:30.475
Marketing may send a message, depending on
the shopper’s consent.

00:02:30.475 --> 00:02:33.900
Neither process controls the truck
carrying her order.

00:02:35.450 --> 00:02:40.250
At the end of this order, analytics can
join the sale, delivery and refund.

00:02:40.250 --> 00:02:42.800
The original sale was eighty dollars.

00:02:42.800 --> 00:02:45.250
The shopper sends back two items.

00:02:45.250 --> 00:02:48.025
Finance needs to know the value of those
items

00:02:48.025 --> 00:02:50.875
and whether the money has gone back to the
customer.

00:02:50.875 --> 00:02:53.250
The warehouse can answer the first part.

00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:59.550
Return one oh four points back to two
lines on the original order.

00:02:59.550 --> 00:03:03.150
The warehouse scans both items at the
receiving dock.

00:03:03.150 --> 00:03:05.150
That proves both arrived.

00:03:05.150 --> 00:03:08.525
The boxes still need inspection before the
shop knows what

00:03:08.525 --> 00:03:11.750
to do with the goods or how much credit to
approve.

00:03:13.258 --> 00:03:16.483
The eighteen-dollar item can go back on
the shelf.

00:03:16.483 --> 00:03:20.558
The twelve-dollar item arrived damaged and
goes to scrap.

00:03:20.558 --> 00:03:24.583
Dynamics calls the decision for each item
its disposition.

00:03:24.583 --> 00:03:27.333
In this case both decisions include a
credit,

00:03:27.333 --> 00:03:29.958
although only one item can be sold again.

00:03:31.467 --> 00:03:34.442
The stock count gains one sellable item.

00:03:34.442 --> 00:03:37.392
The warehouse records one item as scrap.

00:03:37.392 --> 00:03:41.042
Finance now has two approved lines worth
thirty dollars.

00:03:41.042 --> 00:03:43.367
The inspection accounts for the goods.

00:03:43.367 --> 00:03:45.892
A receipt from the payment processor will
show

00:03:45.892 --> 00:03:47.967
when the money went back to the shopper.

00:03:49.883 --> 00:03:54.208
The carrier’s delayed scan, D seven,
carries a tracking number.

00:03:54.208 --> 00:03:56.908
That number points back to order one oh
four.

00:03:56.908 --> 00:04:01.683
The shop’s customer records system
attaches the update to one support case.

00:04:01.683 --> 00:04:05.833
An agent can see what happened to the
parcel as soon as that case updates.

00:04:07.367 --> 00:04:11.792
An order event called E eighty-one arrives
twice after a retry.

00:04:11.792 --> 00:04:13.817
In Microsoft’s reference design,

00:04:13.817 --> 00:04:17.317
an Azure Function checks its ID in Cosmos
DB

00:04:17.317 --> 00:04:20.117
and puts one message on Service Bus.

00:04:20.117 --> 00:04:23.642
Logic Apps starts one Customer Insights
journey.

00:04:23.642 --> 00:04:25.767
The shopper opted out of promotions,

00:04:25.767 --> 00:04:28.392
so the journey sends her no marketing
email.

00:04:29.950 --> 00:04:31.775
At the first reporting cutoff,

00:04:31.775 --> 00:04:35.600
finance has an eighty-dollar sale and no
settled refund.

00:04:35.600 --> 00:04:38.025
The support case is already visible.

00:04:38.025 --> 00:04:41.250
Later the payment receipt arrives for
thirty dollars.

00:04:41.250 --> 00:04:44.750
Finance now reports fifty dollars in
merchandise sales,

00:04:44.750 --> 00:04:47.000
before shipping or tax.

00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:50.425
Each answer reflects the evidence
available at that time.

00:04:52.342 --> 00:04:53.967
The parcel has arrived.

00:04:53.967 --> 00:04:56.517
The customer writes, “My order came late.

00:04:56.517 --> 00:04:58.192
I sent two items back.

00:04:58.192 --> 00:05:03.067
Where’s my refund?” The shop has opened
return one oh four for her order.

00:05:03.067 --> 00:05:06.767
Its support agent runs in Microsoft
Foundry Agent Service

00:05:06.767 --> 00:05:10.742
with instructions, a deployed model and an
order lookup tool.

00:05:12.283 --> 00:05:16.383
The model asks the order lookup tool about
order one oh four.

00:05:16.383 --> 00:05:21.133
The request has a function name and an
argument, the order identifier.

00:05:21.133 --> 00:05:25.733
The shop’s application runs the function
against the merchant’s order system.

00:05:25.733 --> 00:05:29.583
That system can tell whether the warehouse
has received the return.

00:05:31.092 --> 00:05:32.642
The order system replies,

00:05:32.642 --> 00:05:37.142
“Return received.” The agent can say,
“We’ve received your items.

00:05:37.142 --> 00:05:40.192
Your refund is still pending.” The
warehouse can

00:05:40.192 --> 00:05:43.467
inspect a return before the shop approves
a credit.

00:05:43.467 --> 00:05:46.217
The record supports an answer about
receipt.

00:05:46.217 --> 00:05:49.617
A payment confirmation would support an
answer about money.

00:05:51.550 --> 00:05:56.050
The customer attaches an itemized receipt
to the same conversation.

00:05:56.050 --> 00:05:58.375
It lists the two items she sent back,

00:05:58.375 --> 00:06:01.625
one at eighteen dollars and one at twelve.

00:06:01.625 --> 00:06:05.150
The shop supplies the receipt to the
agent’s analysis tool.

00:06:05.150 --> 00:06:07.975
The return number ties that file to her
case.

00:06:09.533 --> 00:06:13.183
Foundry’s Code Interpreter runs Python in
a sandbox,

00:06:13.183 --> 00:06:15.708
an isolated place to run code.

00:06:15.708 --> 00:06:19.458
It can read the attached file and add
those two amounts.

00:06:19.458 --> 00:06:22.008
Thirty dollars is the receipt subtotal.

00:06:22.008 --> 00:06:25.808
A later step decides whether the case
qualifies for a refund.

00:06:27.342 --> 00:06:31.442
The application saves the calculation in
Azure Blob Storage

00:06:31.442 --> 00:06:33.392
and adds a link to the case.

00:06:33.392 --> 00:06:37.417
A reviewer can open the result after the
sandbox session ends.

00:06:37.417 --> 00:06:39.192
The graph can distinguish where the work

00:06:39.192 --> 00:06:41.617
happened from where its evidence remains.

00:06:43.550 --> 00:06:45.450
The conversation ends.

00:06:45.450 --> 00:06:50.125
A queue holds a message with the case
number so work can continue later.

00:06:50.125 --> 00:06:53.550
The live chat can finish even while
another service is waiting

00:06:53.550 --> 00:06:55.025
to pick up the return.

00:06:56.575 --> 00:06:58.775
A review agent picks up the case.

00:06:58.775 --> 00:07:00.900
It reads the saved calculation

00:07:00.900 --> 00:07:04.225
and drafts a summary of the customer’s
explanation.

00:07:04.225 --> 00:07:08.800
A model helps with this step because the
wording varies from case to case.

00:07:08.800 --> 00:07:11.950
Its summary can help a reviewer decide
what to check.

00:07:13.492 --> 00:07:17.492
Once the case has approval, the payout
worker sends the approved amount

00:07:17.492 --> 00:07:20.317
and refund ID to the payment processor.

00:07:20.317 --> 00:07:22.567
Later, a scheduled job compares the

00:07:22.567 --> 00:07:25.367
processor’s receipts with the case
records.

00:07:25.367 --> 00:07:28.142
Both jobs follow fixed procedures.

00:07:28.142 --> 00:07:30.717
Neither needs the model that wrote the
summary.

00:07:32.658 --> 00:07:34.683
Suppose the summary is late.

00:07:34.683 --> 00:07:37.383
A reviewer can still open the original
receipt,

00:07:37.383 --> 00:07:39.633
so the summary is optional here.

00:07:39.633 --> 00:07:42.833
An amber warning marks the delay in case
review.

00:07:42.833 --> 00:07:45.283
An approved payout can still go ahead.

00:07:46.850 --> 00:07:50.675
Now the payout worker’s calls to the
payment processor time out.

00:07:50.675 --> 00:07:55.550
Sending money requires a successful call,
so the payout rule turns red.

00:07:55.550 --> 00:07:59.200
The live order lookup still succeeds and
stays green.

00:07:59.200 --> 00:08:03.600
One customer can get an accurate status
update while her refund is stuck.

00:08:05.158 --> 00:08:08.658
Does the timeout prove that the processor
itself is down?

00:08:08.658 --> 00:08:10.983
The worker recorded a failed call.

00:08:10.983 --> 00:08:14.933
It has no direct readings from inside the
external processor.

00:08:14.933 --> 00:08:17.933
The health model can mark the payout path
Unhealthy

00:08:17.933 --> 00:08:20.733
and leave the processor’s own health
Unknown.

00:08:22.633 --> 00:08:26.908
The shop starts saving return attachments
at a new storage address.

00:08:26.908 --> 00:08:29.833
Its configuration names the destination.

00:08:29.833 --> 00:08:33.108
That setting supplies evidence for a new
dependency,

00:08:33.108 --> 00:08:37.108
even before the Azure inventory contains a
matching account.

00:08:37.108 --> 00:08:40.283
Warehouse and carrier records have their
own paths.

00:08:41.875 --> 00:08:45.125
Azure then reports a storage account at
that address.

00:08:45.125 --> 00:08:48.225
A query can match the setting to the
inventory record

00:08:48.225 --> 00:08:50.400
and connect the app to the account.

00:08:50.400 --> 00:08:54.200
A separate reading can show whether a file
write succeeds.

00:08:54.200 --> 00:08:57.825
Finding a resource and measuring it answer
different questions.

00:08:59.367 --> 00:09:01.942
The model needs words for what changed.

00:09:01.942 --> 00:09:05.167
Its ontology defines kinds of things and
links,

00:09:05.167 --> 00:09:08.717
such as a storage account and a service
that writes to it.

00:09:08.717 --> 00:09:12.892
People can maintain that vocabulary and
refine the health rules.

00:09:12.892 --> 00:09:17.067
A receipt write might be required while a
summary remains optional.

00:09:19.008 --> 00:09:23.208
The old view links the returns app to its
original storage account.

00:09:23.208 --> 00:09:25.708
The new view points to the replacement.

00:09:25.708 --> 00:09:27.808
Each version is a revision.

00:09:27.808 --> 00:09:30.258
A patch lists the changes between them,

00:09:30.258 --> 00:09:34.458
so a past investigation can use the map
that applied at the time.

00:09:35.967 --> 00:09:38.817
The payout warning has a trail of its own.

00:09:38.817 --> 00:09:40.967
The worker recorded the timeout.

00:09:40.967 --> 00:09:45.042
The dependency says that a payout needs a
successful payment call.

00:09:45.042 --> 00:09:47.392
The rule brings those facts together.

00:09:47.392 --> 00:09:51.867
Tracing a conclusion back through the rule
to its evidence is called lineage.

00:09:53.433 --> 00:09:56.033
Order one oh four is an illustration.

00:09:56.033 --> 00:10:00.333
In the companion shop there’s a recording
the Sattle engine can replay.

00:10:00.333 --> 00:10:03.983
At 10:11, the database meter drops to
zero.

00:10:03.983 --> 00:10:07.058
Revision sixteen shows which callers turn
red.

00:10:07.058 --> 00:10:09.183
Two good minutes clear the alarm.

00:10:09.183 --> 00:10:13.008
The recorded inputs and rules let Sattle
reproduce the result.
